# Changelog

This changelog starts at v0.1.0, the first AndeSim release. The history
before v0.1.0 is not reconstructed here.

The format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).

## v0.1.0

The first release. AndeSim gives a cycle count for a marked region of
your own code, on a cycle-accurate model of an Andes RISC-V core.

### Added

- **Three run modes.** `fast` runs the whole program in QEMU. `cycle`
  runs the whole program on the cycle-accurate engine, from reset.
  `hybrid` lets QEMU run up to the start of the region, then measures
  the region on the cycle-accurate engine.
- **Two region triggers.** `--trigger marker` reads the
  `andesim_ROI_begin()` and `andesim_ROI_end()` HINT instructions from
  `andesim/ROI.h`. `--trigger pc` takes a symbol or an address, for a
  program that you cannot change.
- **One image holds everything.** `scripts/build_image.sh` builds an
  image that carries QEMU, the cycle-accurate engine, the handoff
  plugin, the driver and the runtime library. Each source is pinned by
  commit. The build context is empty, so no file from the build machine
  enters the image. Users prepare nothing themselves.
- **A run always happens in that image**, on Linux and on macOS. Both
  legs of a hybrid run must share one Linux kernel. `./andesim` starts
  the container, mounts the current directory at the same path, and
  sizes `/dev/shm` from the `--mem-size` of the run.
- **A bare-metal C runtime** (`build/runtime/`): `printf`, `exit`, file
  I/O and the marker header. Two flags link it:
  `-B build/runtime -specs=andesim.specs`.
- **A Linux runtime** (`--runtime linux`): the vlinux proxy kernel boots
  a static glibc program. See `runtime/linux/README.md`.
- **`andesim profile`** finds the hot loops of a program under QEMU and
  reports the regions that are worth a hybrid measurement.
- **`andesim run --verify`** compares a hybrid run against a QEMU
  oracle.
- **`andesim run --kanata <file>`** writes a pipeline trace of the
  region, for Konata.
- **`andesim --version`** reports the identity of all four bundled
  parts: the driver, the shared ABI, QEMU and each engine. It reads the
  prebuilt binaries themselves, so a mixed install stays visible.
- **`andesim doctor`** reports which artifacts, submodules and tools are
  present.
- **Layered help.** `andesim --help` gives the overview and the
  commands. Each command's own `--help` gives the complete option list
  of that command, with examples. Every option that AndeSim accepts
  appears in the help of the command that accepts it.
- **The documentation set**: this changelog, `README.md`, and
  `runtime/linux/README.md` beside the code that it describes.

### Engines

The image builds one cycle-accurate engine, `ax46mpv_fpga_l3`
(VLEN 1024, ELEN 64, 2 MB L3). An installation that carries other
engines can still use them: `andesim list engines` reports what is
present, and `--engine <id>` selects one.

### Known limitations

- AndeSim supports 64-bit RISC-V only.
- Hybrid mode stops at the end marker. The program does not continue
  after the measured region, so output that comes later never appears.
  Use `--mode fast` to see the whole output of the program.
- Hybrid mode holds the guest RAM in one shared window, so `--mem-size`
  stops just under 16G.
- S-mode and U-mode handoff are not supported.
- v0.1.0 publishes no image. Each user builds one, and the build needs
  read access to the Andes RD-SW repositories.
